<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penyengat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penyengat is a small island off Tanjung Pinang in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau Islands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> viceroys, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-dipertuan-muda">Yang Dipertuan Muda</a>, of the Riau-Lingga Sultanate and in the 19th century a centre of Malay learning, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ali-haji">Raja Ali Haji</a> wrote. Its royal mosque and tombs remain places of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penyengat.jpg" alt="Penyengat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achmad Rabin Taim · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulau_Penyengat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
